---
import DocsLayout from "../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import PageBody from "../components/PageBody.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import Code from "../components/Code.astro";
import { defineSections } from "../lib/sections";
import { route } from "../lib/catalogue";

const S = defineSections([
  { id: "introduction", label: "Introduction", heading: false },
  { id: "reading-theme", label: "Reading theme", heading: "Reading the theme" },
  { id: "changing-theme", label: "Changing theme", heading: "Changing the theme" },
  { id: "creating-theme", label: "Creating a theme" },
  { id: "vibrant", label: "Vibrant emphasis" },
]);
---
<DocsLayout page="themes">
<PageBody sections={S}>
  <Section {...S.introduction}>
        <p class="flow-text">
          Expressive maps Material Design 3 color tokens onto live
          <code>--md-sys-color-*</code> custom properties. Components, buttons, and
          the <a href={route('color')}>color utilities</a> all read those
          live names, so flipping the scheme recolors the page without extra classes.
        </p>
        <p>
          Three rules decide which pair is live, in this order:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            Default: <code>:root</code> and <code>:host</code> set
            <code>color-scheme: light dark</code>, so
            <code>light-dark()</code> follows the OS.
          </li>
          <li>
            <code>:root[theme='auto']</code> is the same follow-OS value,
            written explicitly.
          </li>
          <li>
            <code>:root[theme='light']</code> and
            <code>:root[theme='dark']</code> lock the scheme and override
            the OS.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          The attribute also sets <code>color-scheme</code> so native controls
          follow the same scheme. Each of those three rules is paired with a
          <code>:host([theme='&hellip;'])</code> twin, so a shadow host can be
          pinned to a theme the same way <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> can. Every
          token the sheet declares has that pairing, because the sheet is
          supported as a shadow root's <em>only</em> stylesheet and
          <code>:root</code> matches the document element and nothing else.
        </p>
        <p>
          This site starts as
          <code>&lt;html lang="en" theme="auto"&gt;</code>. The theme control
          in the app bar is a menu: Light, Dark, and Auto. The site writes
          the choice to <code>localStorage</code> and restores it before first
          paint so a reload does not flash the wrong scheme.
        </p>
        <article>
          <p class="title-medium mt-0">These tokens follow the page theme</p>
          <p class="mb-3">
            Change the theme from the app bar menu or the buttons in
            <a href="#changing-theme">Changing theme</a>, and this card, the
            buttons, and the swatches change with it.
          </p>
          <div class="row mb-3">
            <div class="s12 m6">
              <div class="color-swatch primary on-primary-text">.primary</div>
              <div class="color-swatch primary-container on-primary-container-text">.primary-container</div>
              <div class="color-swatch surface-container-highest on-surface-variant-text">.surface-container-highest</div>
            </div>
            <div class="s12 m6">
              <div class="color-swatch secondary on-secondary-text">.secondary</div>
              <div class="color-swatch tertiary on-tertiary-text">.tertiary</div>
              <div class="color-swatch error on-error-text">.error</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <a class="button filled mr-2 mb-2">Filled</a>
          <a class="button tonal mr-2 mb-2">Tonal</a>
          <a class="button outlined mr-2 mb-2">Outlined</a>
        </article>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S["reading-theme"]}>
        <p>
          Put a short inline script in <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>, before the
          stylesheet paints, if you want to restore a saved choice and avoid a
          flash of the wrong scheme. The framework does not run this script.
          The theme switch below saves a choice, but restoring it before first
          paint is the page's job.
        </p>
        <p>
          Without a <code>theme</code> attribute, or with
          <code>theme="auto"</code>, the CSS already follows the OS.
          <code>theme="light"</code> and <code>theme="dark"</code> lock it.
          The snippet below restores a stored choice before first paint.
        </p>
<Code code={`
<script>
  function getTheme() {
    const stored = localStorage.getItem('theme');
    if (stored === 'dark' || stored === 'light' || stored === 'auto') return stored;
    return 'auto';
  }

  function setTheme(theme) {
    document.documentElement.setAttribute('theme', theme);
    localStorage.setItem('theme', theme);
  }

  setTheme(getTheme());
</script>
`} />
        <p>
          Keep this inline rather than in an external file so it runs before
          the first paint. This docs site does that in
          <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>.
        </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S["changing-theme"]}>
        <p>
          Switch schemes by writing the attribute:
        </p>
<Code code={`
document.documentElement.setAttribute('theme', 'dark');
`} />
        <p>
          Or let the <code>Theme</code> behavior do it. A click on any element
          with <code>data-theme-set="light"</code>, <code>"dark"</code> or
          <code>"auto"</code> writes the attribute and saves the choice to
          <code>localStorage</code> under <code>theme</code>, the key the
          snippet above reads. The root bundle starts it on load, and the
          modular entry with <code>Theme.Init()</code>. It marks each theme
          control as current or not: <code>aria-checked</code> on
          <code>menuitemradio</code> and <code>radio</code> roles,
          <code>aria-pressed</code> on anything else.
        </p>
<Code code={`
<div class="button-group" role="group" aria-label="Theme">
  <button type="button" class="button" data-theme-set="light">Light</button>
  <button type="button" class="button" data-theme-set="dark">Dark</button>
  <button type="button" class="button" data-theme-set="auto">Auto</button>
</div>
`} />
        <p>
          From script, <code>Expressive.Theme.set('dark')</code> does the same
          as a click, <code>Expressive.Theme.get()</code> returns the current
          theme, and <code>Expressive.Theme.sync(root)</code> marks theme
          controls rendered after load.
        </p>
        <p>
          This site's app-bar menu predates the behavior and also swaps its
          icon, so it writes the attribute itself:
        </p>
<Code code={`
Expressive.Menu.init(document.getElementById('theme-toggle'), {
  alignment: 'right',
  constrainWidth: false,
  onItemClick: (li) => setTheme(li.dataset.theme)
});
`} />
        <p id="page-theme-toggle">
          <button type="button" class="tonal" data-theme="light">Light</button>
          <button type="button" class="tonal" data-theme="dark">Dark</button>
          <button type="button" class="tonal" data-theme="auto">Auto</button>
        </p>
        <p>
          The same three choices live in the top-right of every docs page.
          A reload keeps the one you picked.
        </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S["creating-theme"]}>
        <p>
          Customize by overriding the Material Design 3 tokens. Do not set
          <code>--background-color</code>, <code>--primary-color</code>, or
          other pre-2.1 names. Expressive does not read them.
        </p>
        <p>
          Three layers exist. <code>tokens/_reference.scss</code> generates the
          tonal ramps from <code>--md-source</code>, then resolves them into the
          pairs (<code>--md-sys-color-primary-light</code>,
          <code>--md-sys-color-primary-dark</code>, and the rest).
          <code>tokens/_theme.scss</code> collapses each pair into the live
          <code>--md-sys-color-*</code> name with <code>light-dark()</code>.
          Components only read the live names.
        </p>
        <p>
          Override the <code>-light</code> and <code>-dark</code> pairs so both
          schemes stay consistent. Load your sheet
          <strong>after</strong> Expressive so equal-specificity
          <code>:root</code> rules win.
        </p>
<Code code={`
:root {
  --md-sys-color-primary-light: #6750a4;
  --md-sys-color-on-primary-light: #ffffff;
  --md-sys-color-primary-container-light: #eaddff;
  --md-sys-color-on-primary-container-light: #21005d;

  --md-sys-color-primary-dark: #d0bcff;
  --md-sys-color-on-primary-dark: #381e72;
  --md-sys-color-primary-container-dark: #4f378b;
  --md-sys-color-on-primary-container-dark: #eaddff;
}
`} />
        <p>
          To restyle only one scheme, set the live names on the attribute
          selector. A bare <code>:root &#123; --md-sys-color-primary: … &#125;</code>
          loses to <code>:root[theme='dark']</code> because the attribute
          rule is more specific.
        </p>
<Code code={`
:root[theme='dark'] {
  --md-sys-color-primary: #d0bcff;
  --md-sys-color-on-primary: #381e72;
  --md-sys-color-surface: #1c1b1f;
  --md-sys-color-on-surface: #e6e1e5;
}
`} />
        <p>
          The same custom properties inherit, so a wrapper can preview a
          palette without touching the rest of the page:
        </p>
        <article style="--md-sys-color-primary: #6750a4; --md-sys-color-on-primary: #ffffff; --md-sys-color-primary-container: #eaddff; --md-sys-color-on-primary-container: #21005d;">
          <p class="title-medium mt-0">Scoped preview</p>
          <p>
            This panel overrides primary on itself. The filled and tonal
            buttons pick up the new tokens; the rest of the page does not.
          </p>
          <a class="button filled mr-2 mb-2">Filled</a>
          <a class="button tonal mr-2 mb-2">Tonal</a>
          <a class="button outlined mr-2 mb-2">Outlined</a>
        </article>
<Code code={`
<article style="
  --md-sys-color-primary: #6750a4;
  --md-sys-color-on-primary: #ffffff;
  --md-sys-color-primary-container: #eaddff;
  --md-sys-color-on-primary-container: #21005d;
">
  <a class="button filled">Filled</a>
</article>
`} />
        <p>
          In Sass, consume the live token. The values are hex, so
          <code>rgba(var(--md-sys-color-primary), 0.06)</code> is invalid and
          the browser drops it. Mix with transparency instead:
        </p>
<Code code={`
.my-surface {
  background-color: var(--md-sys-color-surface);
  color: var(--md-sys-color-on-surface);
}

.my-overlay {
  background-color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--md-sys-color-primary) 6%, transparent);
}
`} />
        <p>
          <code>--md-source</code> is the seed for the generated ramps, and it
          is live. Setting it recolors every generated ramp, and every system
          color that resolves to one, at runtime without a rebuild.
          Expressive ships <code>#c6a0f6</code>, Mist Iris. The palette icon in the
          navbar opens a picker that writes this property and stores the choice
          in <code>localStorage</code> so you can browse the rest of the docs
          in the palette you picked.
        </p>
<Code code={`
:root {
  --md-source: #6750a4;   /* one line; the whole theme follows */
}
`} />
        <p>
          Five of the six ramps are derived this way with
          <code>oklch(from var(--md-source) …)</code>. The
          <strong>error</strong> ramp is not derived. Material fixes the
          error hue because "this went wrong" should
          not change color when you change your brand color.
        </p>
        <p>
          Overriding an individual <code>-light</code>/<code>-dark</code> pair
          still works and still wins over the generated value. Use a pair
          override to change one role and <code>--md-source</code> to change
          the whole palette.
        </p>
        <p>
          The table shows where the shipped seed <code>#c6a0f6</code> lands for
          the roles you will most often replace. These are <em>resolved</em>
          values. The stylesheet ships the <code>oklch()</code> expression and
          the browser computes the color, including gamut-mapping the entries
          whose target chroma sRGB cannot reach. Treat them as accurate to about
          a rounding step, not as literals to paste back in.
        </p>
        <table class="striped">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Token</th>
              <th>Light</th>
              <th>Dark</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>primary</code></td><td>#6e4995</td><td>#d8b2ff</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>on-primary</code></td><td>#ffffff</td><td>#3f1861</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>primary-container</code></td><td>#f5ceff</td><td>#56307a</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>on-primary-container</code></td><td>#2a0048</td><td>#f5ceff</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>secondary</code></td><td>#635a6b</td><td>#ccc1d5</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>secondary-container</code></td><td>#e8ddf2</td><td>#4b4253</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>tertiary</code></td><td>#7a5059</td><td>#e8b7c0</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>error</code></td><td>#ba1a1a</td><td>#ffb4ab</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>surface</code></td><td>#faf8fb</td><td>#090809</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>on-surface</code></td><td>#1c1b1d</td><td>#e3e1e4</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>outline</code></td><td>#7a757c</td><td>#938e96</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>
          <code>error</code> is the one row that does not move when you change
          the seed, because its ramp is not generated.
        </p>
        <p>
          The full list of role utilities lives on the
          <a href={route('color')}>Color</a> page. Pair a fill with its
          <code>on-*</code> text class so contrast stays correct when the
          scheme flips.
        </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.vibrant}>
        <p>
          <em>Vibrant</em> is Material 3 Expressive's emphasis axis. It draws the
          same component on an accent container instead of a neutral surface,
          so it is not a separate component variant. Material ships it as a
          parallel token set per component, such as <code>menus-vibrant</code> and
          <code>toolbar-vibrant</code>, so every component that gains the axis
          restates the same mappings.
        </p>
        <p>
          Expressive makes it a foundation instead. The
          <code>vibrant</code> attribute remaps the surface family of
          <code>--md-sys-color-*</code> roles inside its subtree, and every
          component already reads those roles, so components need no
          per-component styles, and nothing changes until you write the attribute.
        </p>
<Code code={`
<article vibrant>
  <p class="title-medium">Vibrant</p>
  <a class="button filled">Filled</a>
</article>
`} />
        <article vibrant>
          <p class="title-medium mt-0">Vibrant surface</p>
          <p>
            This panel is an ordinary Card, an <code>&lt;article&gt;</code>
            with no vibrant styling of its own anywhere in the sheet. It
            carries the attribute, so the surface and text roles it already
            read resolve to the tertiary container instead. The buttons still
            read <code>primary</code> and keep standing out against it, and
            outlines stay neutral. Flip the theme and it follows.
          </p>
          <a class="button filled mr-2 mb-2">Filled</a>
          <a class="button tonal mr-2 mb-2">Tonal</a>
          <a class="button outlined mr-2 mb-2">Outlined</a>
        </article>
        <p>
          There is one ramp, tertiary, which is what Material uses for the
          axis. Pointing the surfaces at primary or secondary instead would
          hide every component whose own fill is that container: a tonal
          button, a FAB, <code>.toolbar.vibrant</code> itself. If you want a
          different one, set the surface tokens on your own wrapper the way
          <a href="#creating-theme">Creating a theme</a> shows for any token.
        </p>
        <p>
          Inside a vibrant subtree, every <code>surface-container</code> level
          resolves to the one container color, because that is what a vibrant
          surface is in Material, so you cannot tell nested surfaces apart
          there. Put the attribute on the component you want emphasized, not
          on the page. The remap also points at the live role names rather than a
          <code>-light</code>/<code>-dark</code> pair, which is why a runtime
          theme switch still reaches it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Menus and toolbars also have a <code>.vibrant</code> class. Those
          classes are Material's own per-component token sets, with the colors
          that spec gives each one. The attribute is the general axis and works
          on any element. A menu inside a vibrant subtree adopts its own vibrant
          mapping automatically. A selected menu item is filled with
          <code>tertiary-container</code>, the same color the attribute gives
          the menu, so the selection would not show against the surface.
          Material handles that by moving selection to solid
          <code>tertiary</code>, which the class already did.
        </p>
        <p>
          The attribute works in a shadow root too. A sheet loaded there cannot
          reach its own host with a descendant selector, so
          <code>:host([vibrant])</code> is matched alongside it. The token
          mapping carries <code>:host</code> for the same reason.
        </p>
  </Section>
</PageBody>
</DocsLayout>
